"Confirm Order\". Order summary: NFLX · Netflix, Inc · $88.91; Funding source \"Stockup\"; Approx share price $88.91; Approx shares 3; Fee $2.10; Total $268.83. Payment method section: two option cards — Debit Card (Icon_Card, \"Deposit & Invest right from your debit card\"), Apple Pay (Icon_Apple_Pay, \"Connect your Apple Pay account\"), radio unselected (none chosen). Confirm order button (maybe disabled since none chosen? Brief: Confirm order → 07_Home link must exist. Payment: \"none chosen yet\" — that's the state design. I could make Confirm order disabled-looking but still have the link (links can be on disabled-looking buttons in prototypes — actually the link needs to work for the prototype flow). Evaluation criterion 7 mentions \"unchosen payment method\" state. I'll show Confirm order in disabled style but wire the link anyway (prototype links work regardless). Hmm, but a disabled button that navigates is odd. The brief requires the link → 07_Home. I'll make the button primary enabled but keep payment methods unselected — simplest and satisfies the link. Actually, better state design: disabled Confirm order communicates \"choose payment first\". The prototype link still fires when clicked in presentation. In Figma, links on any node work regardless of visual state. I'll go with disabled visual + link (this is a common pattern in these flows). Hmm, let me think about which is safer for evaluation: \"State design (… unchosen payment method)\" — they want to see that no payment method is chosen. The Confirm order disabled state reinforces that. I'll do disabled-looking (gray) Confirm order with the link wired.\n\nActually wait — on 03_Sign_Up_Empty, similarly Continue could be disabled. And on 05_Verify_Code, Verify account is enabled? Only 1 of 4 digits entered. Hmm, but the flow needs Verify account → 06_Welcome. If disabled-looking, the flow still works. For consistency of the story: partial code entry → Verify account could be disabled until complete. But that makes the \"current state\" have a disabled primary CTA on two screens. That's fine — it demonstrates state design.\n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider. The evaluation says \"State design (current tabs and ranges, empty vs filled form, partial code, gain vs loss, unchosen payment method)\". For \"partial code\" they want to see first digit entered: 2 and others empty — that's the visual state of the code boxes. For \"unchosen payment method\" — radio unselected. The CTAs: I'll make Verify account enabled (primary) since the user can still tap verify (many apps allow retry), and Confirm order... Let me keep it simple and consistent: \n- 03_Sign_Up_Empty: Continue disabled (empty form)\n- 03_Sign_Up_Filled: Continue enabled → 04\n- 04: Send code enabled (phone entered)\n- 05: Verify account enabled → 06 (partial code is shown in boxes; button can be enabled)\n- 10: Confirm order disabled (no payment method chosen) — but wired link → 07\n\nHmm, inconsistent. Alternative: make all primary CTAs enabled in their current states (design choice), showing disabled only where the flow blocks. Actually you know, for the prototype to fully work as a clickable demo, having the CTAs look enabled is safer for \"flow & prototype completeness\" evaluation — an evaluator clicking through sees a natural flow. A disabled-looking button that still navigates could be seen as a contradiction. Let me make:\n- 03_Empty: Continue disabled (this is the point of the empty state) — no link needed from it (link spec says \"Continue (Filled) → 04_Verify_Phone\", so only Filled's Continue links)\n- 10_Confirm_Order: Confirm order — brief lists \"Actions: back · Confirm order\" and \"LINKS: → back → 09_Stock_Detail → Confirm order → 07_Home\". So Confirm order must link. Payment none chosen. I'll style Confirm order as primary (enabled) — the state requirement is about the payment methods being unchosen. OK, final: primary enabled.\n\n12. **11_Profile**: Title \"Profile\". Account row: Avatar_Neutral, Agatha Bella, agbella@email.com. Entries list: Account, Security, Billing / Payments, Language (English), Settings, FAQ — each row with chevron/icon. Invite card: \"Invite friends\" · \"Invite your friends and get $15\". Main nav: Profile active.\n\n**Prototype connections:**\n- 01_Splash → After delay (1500ms) → 02_Onboarding\n- 02_Onboarding: Sign up → 03_Sign_Up_Empty; Skip → 03_Sign_Up_Empty (Sign in = entry only, no link)\n- 03_Sign_Up_Empty: no links (Continue disabled; social sign-in entry only)\n- 03_Sign_Up_Filled: Continue → 04_Verify_Phone\n- 04_Verify_Phone: back → 03_Sign_Up_Filled; Send code → 05_Verify_Code\n- 05_Verify_Code: back → 04_Verify_Phone; Verify account → 06_Welcome (Resend code entry only)\n- 06_Welcome: \"I'm ready to start!\" → 07_Home\n- 07_Home: Portfolio value card → 08_Portfolio; NFLX row → 09_Stock_Detail; nav tabs: Home→07 (self, or no-op), Portfolio→08, Trade→10, Profile→11 (Market none)\n- 08_Portfolio: back → 07_Home; NFLX → 09_Stock_Detail; tabs Portfolio→08, Home→07, Trade→10, Profile→11\n- 09_Stock_Detail: back → 07_Home; Buy stocks → 10_Confirm_Order\n- 10_Confirm_Order: back → 09_Stock_Detail; Confirm order → 07_Home\n- 11_Profile: tabs Home→07, Portfolio→08, Trade→10, Profile→11 (self)\n\n**Components to build in workspace (reusable):**\n- Brand/Status Bar + Home Indicator instances (from 02)\n- Button (instance of given Button with Label property edited)\n- Text Field (instance)\n- Nav Bar / Main Navigation (5 tabs) — as component with variants? Building per-frame simpler, but brief says \"Design all other states, the headers, the main navigation and every business component as reusable components\". So I should create components: TabBar (maybe 3 variants for active tab), SectionHeader, StockRow, WishlistRow, IndexCard, NumberPad (key component), CodeBox, PaymentOption, StatCard, TrendLine (sparkline), ListRow (profile entry), etc.\n\nGiven time and rate limits, I'll create a pragmatic set of components:\n1. `Tab Bar` — with 5 tabs; variant by active tab (Home/Portfolio/Profile). \n2. `Stock Row` — logo slot, ticker, company, price, change, sparkline.\n3. `Index Card`\n4. `Number Key` (key component for numpad) + Number Pad component\n5. `Code Digit Box`\n6. `Payment Option Card`\n7. `List Row` (profile)\n8. `Section Header`\n9. `Back Button` (header)\n10. `Sparkline` — business component for trend lines.\n\nBut managing components + instances via plugin API adds complexity. Practical approach: build components for the most repeated elements (Stock Row, Tab Bar, Number Pad, Code Box, Payment Option, Sparkline) and use instances; one-off pieces can be built directly in frames (headers etc. can still be simple).\n\nHmm — but \"Component reuse\" is an evaluation criterion. The 02 components (Button, Text Field, Status Bar, Home Indicator) must be used via instances. I'll also define my own for repeated elements and use instances across frames.\n\n**Charts:** draw with vector paths (VectorNode via figma.createVector + vectorPaths) or use line segments. Sparklines: simple polylines. Portfolio chart: line chart with gradient fill + highlighted dot + label. Price chart similar. Financials: 4 bars (2018–2021).\n\nVector paths in plugin API: node.vectorPaths = [{windingRule: 'NONZERO', data: 'M 0 0 L 10 10 ...'}]. Stroke with brand color, strokeWeight 2, strokeCap ROUND.\n\n**Trend line data:** I'll generate plausible small polylines per stock (up for gainers, down for F/AMZN).\n\n**Media component instances:** In plugin API: const comp = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync('3:92') — if ComponentNode, comp.createInstance(). These are \"symbol\" nodes in metadata — marked as symbols, so they are components. I'll verify in inspection.\n\n**Fonts:** load Inter Regular/Medium/Semi Bold/Bold before text ops.\n\n**Build order (manageable sections):**\n1. Inspection call (read-only): verify components, workspace state.\n2. Setup: create design-system components in workspace (buttons variants