scendant with solid fills — the GROUP 'bell-01' has fills=[]... \n\nOH WAIT. The bug: tint's findAll callback — `c.fills=[S(hex)]` — when the node's fills is [] the condition `.some(...)` is false. So nothing recolored... but the SQUARE appears. Unless — the vector's strokes got recolored to t1 AND... no, stroke recolor would be fine (thin bell outline).\n\nAlternative explanation: the instance renders as square because tint() set fills on the INSTANCE itself. Did the instance have fills? Instance of Icon_Notification — maybe the component 3:155 frame itself has a fill (the component frame, not just the group child)! out.notif shows children but not the component frame's own fills. The component 3:155 is likely a 24×24 frame with fills=[]... but maybe it HAS a fill (like white). If component frame has solid white fill, then instance.fills has SOLID → tint sets instance.fills=[S(t1)] → SOLID DARK SQUARE over the bell! And the bell stroke inside also became t1 → dark on dark → looks like solid square. YES that explains it.\n\nCheck: eye icon: if component frame 3:143 has white/solid fill → tint(eye, t2) → grey square. The screenshot 07 shows grey square ✓. And in 10, Apple Pay icon (3:140) looked fine because I probably tinted it differently or it has internal structure... Actually in 10 the icons in circles looked fine — I didn't tint() those maybe (Payment Option component already had them styled).\n\nFix: for the bell/eye instances: set instance.fills=[] and keep the vector strokes recolored to desired color. So:\n```js\ninst.fills=[];  // remove square bg\n// recolor strokes of vector inside:\ninst.findAll(c=>{if('strokes'in c&&Array.isArray(c.strokes)&&c.strokes.some(s=>s.type==='SOLID'))c.strokes=[S(targetHex)];});\n```\nThat gives dark bell outline on transparent bg ✓ and grey eye ✓.\n\nBut wait — do I need to verify the component frame 3:155 actually has a solid fill? I'll check in the fix call: read bi.fills first; if it contains SOLID → that's the square; se